ions among hereditary, strong and stable radicals. In particular we investigate hereditariness of lower strong and stable radicals. Some facts obtained are related to some results and questions of [2, 6, 7].\n\nAll rings in the paper are associative. Fundamental definitions and properties of radicals may be found in [9]. Definitions of hereditary and strong radicals are used as in Sands [7]. We say that a radical S is left (right) stable if\n\n(ρ): for every ring R and every left (right) ideal I of R it follows S(I)⊆S(R).\n\n* * *\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### Inequalities for the Schatten p-norm II\n\n  *   * Fuad Kittaneh\n  * Journal:\n    Glasgow Mathematical Journal / Volume 29 / Issue 1 / January 1987\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:\n    18 May 2009, pp. 99-104\n  *     * Article\n    *       * You have access Access\n      *     * PDF\n    *     * Export citation\n  * View extract\n\nThis paper is a continuation of [3] in which some inequalities for the Schatten p-norm were considered. The purpose of the present paper is to improve some inequalities in [3] as well as to give more inequalities in the same spirit.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch results for Abstract analysis (https://www.cambridge.org/core/browse-subjects/mathematics/abstract-analysis/listing?pageNum=557)\nciteturn16search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; This paper is a continuation of [3] in which some inequalities for the Schatten p-norm were considered.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFull article: Duality Mapping for Schatten Matrix Norms (https://doi.org/10.1080%2F01630563.2021.1922438)\nciteturn16search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Our approach is based on the analysis of the saturation of the Hölder inequality for Schatten norms. ... Inequalities for the Schatten p-norm III.\n---------------------------------------------